# Orthanc, with the DICOMweb plugin, built from Debian rather than pulled.
#
# ## Why not an image from a registry
#
# `orthanc/orthanc` is a **404 on Docker Hub** — not rate limiting, not a
# transient failure — and `jodogne/orthanc:latest` builds and runs but ships no
# DICOMweb plugin, so `POST /dicom-web/studies` returns 404 against it. Neither
# may be pinned for a Phase 2 that intends to use DICOMweb. Debian bookworm ships
# Orthanc 1.10.1 in its own archive, and `orthanc-dicomweb` is the plugin
# package that makes the DICOMweb routes exist at all.
#
# ## Why `/usr/sbin/Orthanc`
#
# The binary is at `/usr/sbin/Orthanc`. Not `/usr/local/sbin/Orthanc`, which is
# where a source build puts it and where a Dockerfile copied from a source-build
# recipe will look for it and find nothing. `orthanc-tools` — the command line
# utilities — **is not a Debian package**; the image carries the server only, and
# anything that needs `orthanc-storescu` will have to bring its own.
#
# **Orthanc is not integrated into Medarx in Phase 1.** This file builds an
# image; no Medarx component talks to it, and there is no DICOMweb client
# anywhere in the tree. It is versioned now so that Phase 2 wires up a
# configuration that has been read rather than one assembled from memory.

#
# ## Why there is a forwarder, and why it is not `RemoteAccessAllowed: true`
#
# Phase 2 has to reach this archive from a browser, and the browser is on the
# host, so every request arrives at a container interface and Orthanc sees it
# as **remote**. With `RemoteAccessAllowed: false` it answers every one of
# them `401`, which is correct behaviour and useless to us. The two ways out
# are both bad, and the second is bad in a way that is easy to miss.
#
# 1. `RemoteAccessAllowed: true`. In Orthanc 1.10.1 this **automatically
#    enables HTTP authentication**, so the archive ends up behind a credential
#    that has to live somewhere: in a tracked file, or as a default password
#    baked into the image that anybody running the demo can read. A DICOM
#    server reachable without a credential is a finding in itself.
# 2. A forwarder inside the container's own network namespace that hands the
#    published port to Orthanc on **127.0.0.1**. Orthanc still believes every
#    request is local, `RemoteAccessAllowed` stays `false`, and no credential
#    exists to leak. The port is still published to host loopback only, so
#    nothing new is exposed off-host.
#
# This is the same property the rest of the project is built on: do the thing
# at the seam rather than by weakening the thing itself. Do not "simplify" it
# back to option 1 without reading this again.
#
# `MEDARX_DICOMWEB_FORWARD_PORT` exists so the forwarder can be *absent* on
# purpose, which is how the absence is demonstrated rather than asserted: run
# the container with it empty and the identical browser-originated request is
# refused with `401`.
FROM debian:bookworm-slim

RUN apt-get update \
    && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends orthanc orthanc-dicomweb socat \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

COPY entrypoint.sh /usr/local/bin/medarx-orthanc-entrypoint
RUN chmod 0755 /usr/local/bin/medarx-orthanc-entrypoint

# 8042 is Orthanc's own listener; 8043 is the loopback forwarder the published
# port is mapped onto. Both are exposed so `docker run -P` cannot accidentally
# publish one without the other.
EXPOSE 8042 8043

ENTRYPOINT ["/usr/local/bin/medarx-orthanc-entrypoint"]
CMD ["/etc/orthanc/orthanc.json"]
